(date - calendar arithmetic for Vox, in the C locale, under UTC only.

 The library is the exports below, called the way you would actually use
 them. Everything else in this file is a step of one of them - Vox has no
 visibility control, so the rest is exported too, but nothing outside this
 list is meant to be called.

   'the moment now'                                        current moment

   'the moment of' with year and month and 'day of month'   civil to moment
   'the moment at' with year and month and 'day of month'
     and hour and minute and second

   'the year of', 'the month of', 'the day of month of',    moment to parts
   'the hour of', 'the minute of', 'the second of',
   'the weekday of', 'the day of year of', 'the iso week of',
   'the iso week year of', 'the quarter of', 'the century of'

   'the month name of', 'the short month name of',          names
   'the weekday name of', 'the short weekday name of'

   'is a leap year', 'the days in', 'is a valid date',       calendar facts
   'is a valid time'

   'seconds later', 'minutes later', 'hours later',          moving around
   'days later', 'weeks later', 'months later', 'years later'
   'the start of the day of', 'the start of the week of',
   'the start of the month of', 'the start of the year of',
   'the weekday on or after', 'the weekday on or before',
   'the days between'

   'the moment written as', 'the date of',                  writing out
   'the date and time of', 'the rfc 3339 date and time of',
   'the rfc email date of', 'the moment in words of'

   'the moment read from', 'reads as a moment'               reading in

   'the moment described by', 'reads as a description'       relative

 THE REPRESENTATION. A moment is a number: whole seconds since 1970-01-01
 00:00:00 UTC, the same number 'the moment now' hands out and the same
 number GNU date prints for %s. A date with no time of day is the moment
 at midnight. Moments before 1970 are negative and work throughout - the
 calendar is proleptic Gregorian, as GNU's is - so every conversion below
 floors explicitly rather than trusting 'divide' and 'modulo', which
 truncate toward zero.

 TWO DELIBERATE DEPARTURES FROM GNU, both documented in README.md:
   - 'months later' and 'years later' clamp the day of month to the target
     month (31 January plus one month is 28 or 29 February) rather than
     overflowing into March the way GNU's own "+1 month" does.
   - there is no time zone but UTC. A parsed offset is honoured on the way
     in; every write is UTC, with %z always "+0000" and %Z always "UTC".

 Build and consume:
   vox date.vox --shared -o libdate.so
   see date version "0.1" from "./libdate.lib".)

Library date version "0.1".

(--- Floor arithmetic. Vox's 'divide' and 'modulo' truncate toward zero,
 like C's; every divisor used below is positive, and these two make every
 later division floor instead, which is what a moment before 1970 needs. )

To 'floor divide' with a number called dividend and a number called divisor.
  a number called quotient is dividend divide divisor.
  a number called 'the remainder' is dividend modulo divisor.
  If 'the remainder' is not 0 and dividend is less than 0 then, set quotient to quotient subtract 1.
  Return a number, quotient.

To 'floor modulo' with a number called dividend and a number called divisor.
  a number called 'the remainder' is dividend modulo divisor.
  If 'the remainder' is less than 0 then, set 'the remainder' to 'the remainder' add divisor.
  Return a number, 'the remainder'.

(--- The civil calendar core: Howard Hinnant's days_from_civil and
 civil_from_days, exact for the proleptic Gregorian calendar with nothing
 but integer arithmetic. A month outside 1..12 is carried into the year
 first, the way 'the moment at' wants (month 13 is January next year); a
 day outside the month's own range is deliberately left alone, because the
 formula below is linear in the day and answers correctly for any integer
 day without that carry - which is what makes day 0 the last day of the
 previous month and day 32 of January 1 February, for free. )

(Month, carried into the year so the result is always 1..12. Takes month
 as any integer and answers the year it lands in and the 1..12 month
 within it, through a map so both come back at once.)
To 'read a normalised month into' with a number called 'the month' and a number called 'the year' and a map called civil.
  a number called 'extra years' is 'floor divide' with {'the month' subtract 1} and 12.
  a number called 'the normalised month' is {'floor modulo' with {'the month' subtract 1} and 12} add 1.
  Set civil's "year" to 'the year' add 'extra years'.
  Set civil's "month" to 'the normalised month'.

(Days since 1970-01-01 for a year, a month (any integer - normalised
 here), and a day (any integer, left exactly as given).)
To 'days from civil' with a number called 'the year' and a number called 'the month' and a number called 'the day'.
  a map called normalised is {}.
  'read a normalised month into' with 'the month' and 'the year' and normalised.
  a number called 'the civil year' is normalised's "year".
  a number called 'the civil month' is normalised's "month".
  If 'the civil month' is less than or equal to 2 then, set 'the civil year' to 'the civil year' subtract 1.
  a number called era is 'floor divide' with 'the civil year' and 400.
  a number called 'the year of the era' is 'the civil year' subtract {era multiply 400}.
  a number called 'the month index' is 'the civil month' subtract 3.
  If 'the civil month' is less than or equal to 2 then, set 'the month index' to 'the civil month' add 9.
  a number called 'the day of the year before the era' is 153 multiply 'the month index'.
  a number called 'the day of the year' is 'floor divide' with {'the day of the year before the era' add 2} and 5.
  set 'the day of the year' to 'the day of the year' add 'the day' subtract 1.
  a number called 'the leap days in the era' is {'floor divide' with 'the year of the era' and 4} subtract {'floor divide' with 'the year of the era' and 100}.
  a number called 'the day of the era' is {'the year of the era' multiply 365} add 'the leap days in the era' add 'the day of the year'.
  Return a number, {era multiply 146097} add 'the day of the era' subtract 719468.

(The inverse: days since 1970-01-01 back to a year, a month (1..12), and
 a day (1..31), written into the civil map's "year", "month", "day".)
To 'read civil into' with a number called 'the day number' and a map called civil.
  a number called z is 'the day number' add 719468.
  a number called era is 'floor divide' with z and 146097.
  a number called 'the day of the era' is z subtract {era multiply 146097}.
  a number called 'the leap correction' is {'floor divide' with 'the day of the era' and 1460} subtract {'floor divide' with 'the day of the era' and 36524}.
  set 'the leap correction' to 'the leap correction' add {'floor divide' with 'the day of the era' and 146096}.
  a number called 'the year of the era guess' is 'floor divide' with {'the day of the era' subtract 'the leap correction'} and 365.
  a number called 'the civil year' is 'the year of the era guess' add {era multiply 400}.
  a number called 'the leap days in the era' is {'floor divide' with 'the year of the era guess' and 4} subtract {'floor divide' with 'the year of the era guess' and 100}.
  a number called 'the day of the year' is 'the day of the era' subtract {{365 multiply 'the year of the era guess'} add 'the leap days in the era'}.
  a number called 'the month index' is 'floor divide' with {{5 multiply 'the day of the year'} add 2} and 153.
  a number called 'the day of the month before adjustment' is 'floor divide' with {{153 multiply 'the month index'} add 2} and 5.
  a number called 'the civil day' is {'the day of the year' subtract 'the day of the month before adjustment'} add 1.
  a number called 'the civil month' is 'the month index' add 3.
  If 'the month index' is greater than or equal to 10 then, set 'the civil month' to 'the month index' subtract 9.
  If 'the civil month' is less than or equal to 2 then, set 'the civil year' to 'the civil year' add 1.
  Set civil's "year" to 'the civil year'.
  Set civil's "month" to 'the civil month'.
  Set civil's "day" to 'the civil day'.

(--- Now ---)

To 'the moment now'.
  Return a number, current time's unix.

(--- Civil time to a moment ---)

To 'the moment of' with a number called 'the year' and a number called 'the month' and a number called 'the day of month'.
  Return a number, {'the moment at' with 'the year' and 'the month' and 'the day of month' and 0 and 0 and 0}.

(Fields that overflow normalise the way C's mktime does - day 0 is the
 last day of the previous month, hour 24 is midnight tomorrow - because
 the day and the time of day are both carried through as plain linear
 offsets from midnight of the (month-normalised) civil date, with nothing
 clamped or range-checked. That is deliberate: see the plan.)
To 'the moment at' with a number called 'the year' and a number called 'the month' and a number called 'the day of month' and a number called 'the hour' and a number called 'the minute' and a number called 'the second'.
  a number called 'the day number' is 'days from civil' with 'the year' and 'the month' and 'the day of month'.
  a number called 'the seconds of the day' is {'the hour' multiply 3600} add {'the minute' multiply 60}.
  set 'the seconds of the day' to 'the seconds of the day' add 'the second'.
  Return a number, {'the day number' multiply 86400} add 'the seconds of the day'.

(--- A moment to its parts ---)

To 'the year of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the day number' is 'floor divide' with moment and 86400.
  a map called civil is {}.
  'read civil into' with 'the day number' and civil.
  Return a number, civil's "year".

To 'the month of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the day number' is 'floor divide' with moment and 86400.
  a map called civil is {}.
  'read civil into' with 'the day number' and civil.
  Return a number, civil's "month".

To 'the day of month of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the day number' is 'floor divide' with moment and 86400.
  a map called civil is {}.
  'read civil into' with 'the day number' and civil.
  Return a number, civil's "day".

To 'the hour of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the total hours' is 'floor divide' with moment and 3600.
  Return a number, 'floor modulo' with 'the total hours' and 24.

To 'the minute of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the total minutes' is 'floor divide' with moment and 60.
  Return a number, 'floor modulo' with 'the total minutes' and 60.

To 'the second of' of a number called moment.
  Return a number, 'floor modulo' with moment and 60.

(1 January 1970 was a Thursday, so the weekday in the 1..7 Monday form is
 the day number plus three, modulo seven, plus one, once the modulo is
 made non-negative.)
To 'the weekday of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the day number' is 'floor divide' with moment and 86400.
  a number called 'the weekday index' is 'floor modulo' with {'the day number' add 3} and 7.
  Return a number, 'the weekday index' add 1.

To 'the day of year of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the day number' is 'floor divide' with moment and 86400.
  a map called civil is {}.
  'read civil into' with 'the day number' and civil.
  a number called 'the year' is civil's "year".
  a number called 'the start of the year' is 'days from civil' with 'the year' and 1 and 1.
  Return a number, {'the day number' subtract 'the start of the year'} add 1.

(How many ISO weeks are in a given Gregorian year: 53 when 1 January
 falls on a Thursday, or the year is a leap year and 1 January falls on
 a Wednesday - the closed form below, from the ISO week date rule.)
To 'the iso week correction for' of a number called 'the year'.
  a number called 'the leap correction' is {'floor divide' with 'the year' and 4} subtract {'floor divide' with 'the year' and 100}.
  set 'the leap correction' to 'the leap correction' add {'floor divide' with 'the year' and 400}.
  Return a number, 'floor modulo' with {'the year' add 'the leap correction'} and 7.

To 'weeks in the iso year' of a number called 'the year'.
  a number called p is 'the iso week correction for' of 'the year'.
  a number called 'p of the previous year' is 'the iso week correction for' of {'the year' subtract 1}.
  If p is 4 then, Return a number, 53.
  If 'p of the previous year' is 3 then, Return a number, 53.
  Return a number, 52.

(The ISO week and its year, written into a map's "week" and "year": a 1
 January can belong to the last week of the previous year, and a 31
 December to week 1 of the next, so the two travel together.)
(week and 'the weeks in this year' cannot both miss - one is less than 1
 only when the other cannot be exceeded - so two plain ifs, checked
 against the year's own week count before either can adjust it, do the
 same job as an if/otherwise without one clause nesting inside the
 other.)
To 'read the iso week into' with a number called moment and a map called 'the result'.
  a number called 'the day number' is 'floor divide' with moment and 86400.
  a map called civil is {}.
  'read civil into' with 'the day number' and civil.
  a number called 'the year' is civil's "year".
  a number called weekday is 'the weekday of' of moment.
  a number called 'the day of year' is 'the day of year of' of moment.
  a number called week is 'floor divide' with {{'the day of year' subtract weekday} add 10} and 7.
  a number called 'the unadjusted week' is week.
  a number called 'the weeks in this year' is 'weeks in the iso year' of 'the year'.
  If 'the unadjusted week' is less than 1 then, set 'the year' to 'the year' subtract 1, set week to 'weeks in the iso year' of 'the year'.
  If 'the unadjusted week' is greater than 'the weeks in this year' then, set 'the year' to 'the year' add 1, set week to 1.
  Set 'the result's "year" to 'the year'.
  Set 'the result's "week" to week.

To 'the iso week of' of a number called moment.
  a map called 'the result' is {}.
  'read the iso week into' with moment and 'the result'.
  Return a number, 'the result's "week".

To 'the iso week year of' of a number called moment.
  a map called 'the result' is {}.
  'read the iso week into' with moment and 'the result'.
  Return a number, 'the result's "year".

To 'the quarter of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the month' is 'the month of' of moment.
  Return a number, {'floor divide' with {'the month' subtract 1} and 3} add 1.

To 'the century of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the year' is 'the year of' of moment.
  Return a number, 'floor divide' with 'the year' and 100.

(--- Names. A name list, indexed 1..N: out of range is an out-of-bounds
 list read into a text destination, which is the empty text - exactly the
 answer the plan asks for, for free. )

To 'the month name of' of a number called 'the month'.
  a list called names is ["January", "February", "March", "April", "May", "June", "July", "August", "September", "October", "November", "December"].
  a text called name is element 'the month' of names.
  Return a text, name.

To 'the short month name of' of a number called 'the month'.
  a list called names is ["Jan", "Feb", "Mar", "Apr", "May", "Jun", "Jul", "Aug", "Sep", "Oct", "Nov", "Dec"].
  a text called name is element 'the month' of names.
  Return a text, name.

To 'the weekday name of' of a number called weekday.
  a list called names is ["Monday", "Tuesday", "Wednesday", "Thursday", "Friday", "Saturday", "Sunday"].
  a text called name is element weekday of names.
  Return a text, name.

To 'the short weekday name of' of a number called weekday.
  a list called names is ["Mon", "Tue", "Wed", "Thu", "Fri", "Sat", "Sun"].
  a text called name is element weekday of names.
  Return a text, name.

(--- Calendar facts ---)

(A raw comparison cannot itself sit on the right of a declaration's own
 "is" - the two "is"es collide - so a leap year is decided with plain
 Ifs and numbers rather than a chain of named booleans.)
To 'is a leap year' of a number called 'the year'.
  a number called 'divides by four' is 'floor modulo' with 'the year' and 4.
  a number called 'divides by a hundred' is 'floor modulo' with 'the year' and 100.
  a number called 'divides by four hundred' is 'floor modulo' with 'the year' and 400.
  If 'divides by four' is not 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
  If 'divides by a hundred' is not 0 then, Return a boolean, true.
  Return a boolean, 'divides by four hundred' is 0.

To 'the days in' of a number called 'the month' and a number called 'the year'.
  a list called lengths is [31, 28, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31].
  a number called 'the length' is element 'the month' of lengths.
  a boolean called 'it is a leap february' is false.
  If 'the month' is 2 and {'is a leap year' of 'the year'} then, set 'it is a leap february' to true.
  If 'it is a leap february' then, set 'the length' to 29.
  Return a number, 'the length'.

To 'is a valid date' of a number called 'the year' and a number called 'the month' and a number called 'the day of month'.
  If 'the month' is less than 1 then, Return a boolean, false.
  If 'the month' is greater than 12 then, Return a boolean, false.
  If 'the day of month' is less than 1 then, Return a boolean, false.
  a number called 'the length' is 'the days in' of 'the month' and 'the year'.
  Return a boolean, 'the day of month' is less than or equal to 'the length'.

To 'is a valid time' of a number called 'the hour' and a number called 'the minute' and a number called 'the second'.
  If 'the hour' is less than 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
  If 'the hour' is greater than 23 then, Return a boolean, false.
  If 'the minute' is less than 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
  If 'the minute' is greater than 59 then, Return a boolean, false.
  If 'the second' is less than 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
  Return a boolean, 'the second' is less than or equal to 59.

(--- Moving around the calendar. The plain ones are arithmetic on the
 moment itself - a moment is already a count of seconds, so "later" is
 just adding more of them. )

To 'seconds later' of a number called moment and a number called count.
  Return a number, moment add count.

To 'minutes later' of a number called moment and a number called count.
  Return a number, moment add {count multiply 60}.

To 'hours later' of a number called moment and a number called count.
  Return a number, moment add {count multiply 3600}.

To 'days later' of a number called moment and a number called count.
  Return a number, moment add {count multiply 86400}.

To 'weeks later' of a number called moment and a number called count.
  Return a number, moment add {count multiply 604800}.

(Month and year arithmetic clamp the day of month to the target month
 instead of overflowing into the month after, which is GNU's own "+1
 month" - see the header comment and README.md.)
To 'months later' of a number called moment and a number called count.
  a map called civil is {}.
  a number called 'the day number' is 'floor divide' with moment and 86400.
  'read civil into' with 'the day number' and civil.
  a number called 'the year' is civil's "year".
  a number called 'the month' is civil's "month".
  a number called 'the day of month' is civil's "day".
  a number called 'the total months' is {{'the year' multiply 12} add {'the month' subtract 1}} add count.
  a number called 'the target year' is 'floor divide' with 'the total months' and 12.
  a number called 'the target month' is {'floor modulo' with 'the total months' and 12} add 1.
  a number called 'the longest day' is 'the days in' of 'the target month' and 'the target year'.
  If 'the day of month' is greater than 'the longest day' then, set 'the day of month' to 'the longest day'.
  a number called 'the hour' is 'the hour of' of moment.
  a number called 'the minute' is 'the minute of' of moment.
  a number called 'the second' is 'the second of' of moment.
  Return a number, {'the moment at' with 'the target year' and 'the target month' and 'the day of month' and 'the hour' and 'the minute' and 'the second'}.

To 'years later' of a number called moment and a number called count.
  Return a number, {'months later' of moment and {count multiply 12}}.

(--- Anchors ---)

To 'the start of the day of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the day number' is 'floor divide' with moment and 86400.
  Return a number, 'the day number' multiply 86400.

To 'the start of the week of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the start of the day' is 'the start of the day of' of moment.
  a number called weekday is 'the weekday of' of moment.
  Return a number, 'the start of the day' subtract {{weekday subtract 1} multiply 86400}.

To 'the start of the month of' of a number called moment.
  a map called civil is {}.
  a number called 'the day number' is 'floor divide' with moment and 86400.
  'read civil into' with 'the day number' and civil.
  a number called 'the year' is civil's "year".
  a number called 'the month' is civil's "month".
  Return a number, {'the moment of' with 'the year' and 'the month' and 1}.

To 'the start of the year of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the year' is 'the year of' of moment.
  Return a number, {'the moment of' with 'the year' and 1 and 1}.

To 'the weekday on or after' of a number called moment and a number called weekday.
  a number called 'the start of the day' is 'the start of the day of' of moment.
  a number called 'the current weekday' is 'the weekday of' of moment.
  a number called 'the difference' is 'floor modulo' with {weekday subtract 'the current weekday'} and 7.
  Return a number, 'the start of the day' add {'the difference' multiply 86400}.

To 'the weekday on or before' of a number called moment and a number called weekday.
  a number called 'the start of the day' is 'the start of the day of' of moment.
  a number called 'the current weekday' is 'the weekday of' of moment.
  a number called 'the difference' is 'floor modulo' with {'the current weekday' subtract weekday} and 7.
  Return a number, 'the start of the day' subtract {'the difference' multiply 86400}.

To 'the days between' of a number called 'the earlier' and a number called 'the later'.
  Return a number, 'floor divide' with {'the later' subtract 'the earlier'} and 86400.

(--- Writing a moment out. The layout language is GNU's %-sequences,
 walked one byte at a time into a growing buffer. )

To 'write the byte' with a number called 'the byte to write' and a buffer called output.
  set byte {output's size add 1} of output to 'the byte to write'.

To 'uppercase text' of a text called words.
  a buffer called letters is "{words}".
  Create a buffer called upper.
  a number called 'the length' is letters's size.
  a number called position is 1.
  a number called 'the byte' is 0.
  While position is less than or equal to 'the length',
    set 'the byte' to byte position of letters,
    if 'the byte' is greater than or equal to 'a' and 'the byte' is less than or equal to 'z' then, set 'the byte' to 'the byte' subtract 32.
    'write the byte' with 'the byte' and upper,
    increment position.
  Return a text, "{upper}".

To 'lowercase text' of a text called words.
  a buffer called letters is "{words}".
  Create a buffer called lower.
  a number called 'the length' is letters's size.
  a number called position is 1.
  a number called 'the byte' is 0.
  While position is less than or equal to 'the length',
    set 'the byte' to byte position of letters,
    if 'the byte' is greater than or equal to 'A' and 'the byte' is less than or equal to 'Z' then, set 'the byte' to 'the byte' add 32.
    'write the byte' with 'the byte' and lower,
    increment position.
  Return a text, "{lower}".

(GNU's ^ (upper case if possible) and # (opposite case if possible),
 restricted to the two shapes the layout's own names actually take: a
 title-cased name (a short or long month or weekday name), where both
 flags mean "upper case", and an all-upper-case one (%p, %Z), where ^ is a
 no-op and # means "lower case". %P is neither: GNU's own %P ignores both
 flags, which 'write a specifier' below honours by never calling either
 of these for it.)
To 'cased for a title' with a text called raw and a number called 'case flag'.
  If 'case flag' is '^' then, Return a text, {'uppercase text' of raw}.
  If 'case flag' is '#' then, Return a text, {'uppercase text' of raw}.
  Return a text, raw.

To 'cased for upper' with a text called raw and a number called 'case flag'.
  If 'case flag' is '#' then, Return a text, {'lowercase text' of raw}.
  Return a text, raw.

(raw, right-aligned to width with 'the pad byte', unless 'suppress
 padding' says not to bother. A leading '-' stays first, ahead of the
 padding, the way a signed number is written.)
To 'write padded' with a buffer called output and a text called raw and a number called width and a number called 'the pad byte' and a boolean called 'suppress padding'.
  a buffer called letters is "{raw}".
  a number called 'the length' is letters's size.
  a number called 'the sign length' is 0.
  a number called 'the first byte' is 0.
  If 'the length' is greater than 0 then, set 'the first byte' to byte 1 of letters.
  If 'the first byte' is '-' then, set 'the sign length' to 1.
  a number called 'the pad count' is width subtract 'the length'.
  If 'suppress padding' then, set 'the pad count' to 0.
  If 'the pad count' is less than 0 then, set 'the pad count' to 0.
  If 'the sign length' is 1 then, 'write the byte' with '-' and output.
  Repeat 'the pad count' times, 'write the byte' with 'the pad byte' and output.
  For each position from {1 add 'the sign length'} to 'the length', 'write the byte' with {byte position of letters} and output.

(The default width and pad byte, unless the layout asked for its own.)
To 'render specifier' with a buffer called output and a text called raw and a number called 'the default width' and a number called 'the default pad' and a number called 'pad flag' and a number called width.
  a number called 'the width to use' is 'the default width'.
  If width is greater than or equal to 0 then, set 'the width to use' to width.
  a number called 'the pad to use' is 'the default pad'.
  If 'pad flag' is '0' then, set 'the pad to use' to '0'.
  If 'pad flag' is '_' then, set 'the pad to use' to ' '.
  a boolean called 'suppress padding' is false.
  If 'pad flag' is '-' then, set 'suppress padding' to true.
  'write padded' with output and raw and 'the width to use' and 'the pad to use' and 'suppress padding'.

To 'the twelve hour of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the hour' is 'the hour of' of moment.
  a number called h is 'floor modulo' with 'the hour' and 12.
  If h is 0 then, Return a number, 12.
  Return a number, h.

To 'the am or pm of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the hour' is 'the hour of' of moment.
  If 'the hour' is less than 12 then, Return a text, "AM".
  Return a text, "PM".

(%U: Sunday-first week, 00 before the year's first Sunday. %W: the same
 with Monday first. Both are floor((yday0 + 7 - wday) / 7), 0-indexed,
 with wday counted from the matching first day.)
To 'the sunday week of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the day of year zero based' is {'the day of year of' of moment} subtract 1.
  a number called 'the sunday based weekday' is 'the day of week sunday based of' of moment.
  a number called 'the numerator' is {'the day of year zero based' add 7} subtract 'the sunday based weekday'.
  Return a number, 'floor divide' with 'the numerator' and 7.

To 'the monday week of' of a number called moment.
  a number called 'the day of year zero based' is {'the day of year of' of moment} subtract 1.
  a number called 'the monday based weekday' is {'the weekday of' of moment} subtract 1.
  a number called 'the numerator' is {'the day of year zero based' add 7} subtract 'the monday based weekday'.
  Return a number, 'floor divide' with 'the numerator' and 7.

To 'the day of week sunday based of' of a number called moment.
  Return a number, 'floor modulo' with {'the weekday of' of moment} and 7.

(One %-sequence, whatever letter it turns out to be: every field the
 layout table lists, computed once so the table below is just a lookup.
 Answers whether spec was a sequence this library knows; 'write one
 escape at' below is what falls back to a bare '%' when it says no.)
To 'write a specifier' with a buffer called output and a number called moment and a number called spec and a number called 'pad flag' and a number called 'case flag' and a number called width.
  a number called 'the year' is 'the year of' of moment.
  a number called 'the month' is 'the month of' of moment.
  a number called 'the day of month' is 'the day of month of' of moment.
  a number called 'the hour' is 'the hour of' of moment.
